":{"red":0.09411764705882353,"green":0.19215686274509805,"blue":0.2235294117647059}}}},"fields":"fontFamily,fontSize,bold,foregroundColor,link"}},{"updateParagraphStyle":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_23","textRange":{"type":"ALL"},"style":{"alignment":"START","lineSpacing":110,"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":5,"unit":"PT"},"indentStart":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentEnd":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"alignment,lineSpacing,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,indentStart,indentEnd,indentFirstLine"}},{"deleteText":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_26","textRange":{"type":"ALL"}}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_26","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Initial estimate held fixed\nEstimate updated from interaction\nTrue mass supplied"}},{"updatePageElementTransform":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_26","applyMode":"ABSOLUTE","transform":{"scaleX":1.3224933333333333,"scaleY":0.6112933333333334,"translateX":378.8,"translateY":169.8,"unit":"PT"}}},{"updateTextStyle":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_26","textRange":{"type":"ALL"},"style":{"fontFamily":"Arial","fontSize":{"magnitude":14.4,"unit":"PT"},"bold":false,"foregroundColor":{"opaqueColor":{"rgbColor":{"red":0.09411764705882353,"green":0.19215686274509805,"blue":0.2235294117647059}}}},"fields":"fontFamily,fontSize,bold,foregroundColor,link"}},{"updateParagraphStyle":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_26","textRange":{"type":"ALL"},"style":{"alignment":"START","lineSpacing":110,"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":5,"unit":"PT"},"indentStart":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentEnd":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"alignment,lineSpacing,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,indentStart,indentEnd,indentFirstLine"}},{"deleteText":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_27","textRange":{"type":"ALL"}}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_27","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Measure estimation error and task success under matched interaction budgets."}},{"updatePageElementTransform":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_27","applyMode":"ABSOLUTE","transform":{"scaleX":2.80416,"scaleY":0.17526,"translateX":28.8,"translateY":318.8,"unit":"PT"}}},{"updateTextStyle":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_27","textRange":{"type":"ALL"},"style":{"fontFamily":"Arial","fontSize":{"magnitude":13.5,"unit":"PT"},"bold":false,"foregroundColor":{"opaqueColor":{"rgbColor":{"red":0.09411764705882353,"green":0.19215686274509805,"blue":0.2235294117647059}}}},"fields":"fontFamily,fontSize,bold,foregroundColor,link"}},{"updateParagraphStyle":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_27","textRange":{"type":"ALL"},"style":{"alignment":"START","lineSpacing":110,"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":5,"unit":"PT"},"indentStart":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentEnd":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"alignment,lineSpacing,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,indentStart,indentEnd,indentFirstLine"}},{"createShape":{"objectId":"pi_reframe_later_work","shapeType":"TEXT_BOX","elementProperties":{"pageObjectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_14","size":{"width":{"magnitude":662.4,"unit":"PT"},"height":{"magnitude":41.4,"unit":"PT"}},"transform":{"scaleX":1,"scaleY":1,"translateX":28.8,"translateY":357.8,"unit":"PT"}}}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"pi_reframe_later_work","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Later: CREST, broader materials and movement conditions, and learning across experience."}},{"updateTextStyle":{"objectId":"pi_reframe_later_work","textRange":{"type":"ALL"},"style":{"fontFamily":"Arial","fontSize":{"magnitude":13.5,"unit":"PT"},"bold":false,"foregroundColor":{"opaqueColor":{"rgbColor":{"red":0.09411764705882353,"green":0.19215686274509805,"blue":0.2235294117647059}}}},"fields":"fontFamily,fontSize,bold,foregroundColor,link"}},{"updateParagraphStyle":{"objectId":"pi_reframe_later_work","textRange":{"type":"ALL"},"style":{"alignment":"START","lineSpacing":110,"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":5,"unit":"PT"},"indentStart":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentEnd":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"alignment,lineSpacing,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,indentStart,indentEnd,indentFirstLine"}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"SLIDES_API1743411364_1","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Updated research framing: the broad goal is deployment adaptation of an existing manipulation skill. For this study, physical understanding is operationalized as estimates of task-relevant physical properties. Movement capability is an enabling condition; the research focus is how observation and interaction improve the physical context available to the policy. Successes, failures and unexpected responses are evidence, not unique causal labels. Initial mass estimation is the first implementation. Recognizing previously unmodeled causes is a longer-term question, not an achieved result.\n\nEarlier notes retained below:\n"}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"SLIDES_API1743411364_3","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Enabling capability: a controller must first be able to succeed in the tested physical conditions, and its behavior should depend usefully on the supplied context. RL training alone does not guarantee this. The planned height-generalization study remains future work: train across initial object heights and evaluate held-out heights. Separate interpolation from extrapolation and keep context accuracy distinct from policy capability.\n\nEarlier notes retained below:\n"}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"SLIDES_API1743411364_5","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Completed visual-mass baseline: one material, fixed density/friction/camera, size variation. MAE is 2.76 g on 12 held-out sizes and 48 images, against a constant baseline of 106.37 g. This supports initial estimation in the controlled distribution, not adaptation during deployment or arbitrary-material mass inference. Source memo retained from the original slide: https://github.com/Spadeyache/adaptability ; runs/visual_mass/initial_rgb_mass/model/metrics.json and predictions.png.\n\nEarlier notes retained below:\n"}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"SLIDES_API1743411364_7","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Current pipeline is a completed integration demonstration, not a learned online update. One raw initial RGB image is passed through the frozen mass estimator before contact. Mass is converted to supplied weight for the frozen policy. The policy receives robot/object state and finger normal-compression signals throughout execution. Both model weights and mass estimate remain fixed. Representative 5 cm cube: 125 g actual, 121.24 g estimated, 1.19 N supplied; 50 steps. Ground-truth mass is for evaluation only.\n\nEarlier notes retained below:\n"}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"SLIDES_API540505727_1","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Proposed research direction, not implemented: use observations and execution outcomes to revise task-relevant physical beliefs. Updating a current estimate is state/parameter inference; it need not change estimator weights. A shared learned updater could maintain a separate evolving belief for each encounter. Improving shared estimator weights across experiences is a distinct later choice. When current properties cannot explain an outcome, detecting model inadequacy, identifying a useful additional factor, and learning a correction are separate longer-term problems. An unexpected outcome alone does not prove a novel failure cause.\n\nEarlier notes retained below:\n"}},{"insertText":{"objectId":"h437c0d080b9bf084_0_18","insertionIndex":0,"text":"Proposed first controlled test: one cube geometry and appearance, fixed friction/pose and a frozen policy. Vary actual mass and initial estimation error. Before training an updater, check that the policy is capable under the chosen masses and that accurate supplied mass usefully changes performance. Check that permitted action/observation histories distinguish mass; finger normal compression is not vertical load, and failure alone does not identify mass. Compare fixed initial estimate, interaction-updated estimate and true-mass reference under matched probing and task budgets. True mass is an information reference, not a guaranteed performance ceiling. Keep evaluation mass out of deployment features. See presentations/interaction_adaptation_experiment.md for the proposed study. Related reading retained from the original draft: https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.09180 (lifelong multi-task RL, background reading rather than the estimator-update method).\n\nEarlier notes retained below:\n"}}]